    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "Wave optics is switched on (scene setting waveMode), so narrow openings diffract and detectors add light fields instead of powers. Top row (y = 0): Young's experiment - a 2 mm HeNe beam (632.8 nm) covers two 0.04 mm slits 0.25 mm apart; the screen 500 mm behind shows cos^2 fringes with spacing lambda L / d = 1.27 mm under the wide single-slit envelope. Middle row (y = 130): one 0.1 mm slit and a 1024-pixel line camera - the sinc^2 pattern with its first dark band at lambda L / a = 3.16 mm from the centre and side lobes of 4.7 %. Bottom row (y = 260): five slits 0.1 mm apart - principal maxima lambda L / d = 3.16 mm apart, each far sharper than a two-slit fringe (width ~ 1/N of the spacing), with three faint subsidiary maxima in between: the beginning of a diffraction grating. Try changing the slit separation, the wavelength or the number of slits, or switch waveMode off to see the purely geometric shadows.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Double Slit, Single Slit & Grating Slits (wave optics)"
  },
